1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,550
Why is it so hard to tell how little we know?

2
00:00:14,237 --> 00:00:16,337
One of your first games of chess.

3
00:00:16,417 --> 00:00:17,730
You play black.

4
00:00:17,845 --> 00:00:22,085
Three moves in, white has already brought out its queen and a bishop.

5
00:00:22,403 --> 00:00:23,728
Then you spot it.

6
00:00:23,796 --> 00:00:26,410
Move the knight here, and it attacks the queen.

7
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:27,412
What a move!

8
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:29,157
You are sure of it.

9
00:00:29,568 --> 00:00:30,893
So you play it.

10
00:00:30,947 --> 00:00:33,591
And one move later, the queen takes this pawn,

11
00:00:33,631 --> 00:00:36,393
with the bishop guarding her from across the board.

12
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:37,558
Checkmate.

13
00:00:38,001 --> 00:00:40,526
The danger sat on the board the whole time.

14
00:00:40,566 --> 00:00:42,904
To your eyes, it was not there.

15
00:00:43,302 --> 00:00:47,093
The less you know, the harder it is to see how little you know.

16
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:50,471
That is the idea behind the Dunning-Kruger effect.

17
00:00:52,158 --> 00:00:56,893
Now picture everything there is to know about a subject as a dark field.

18
00:00:56,933 --> 00:01:00,958
What you have learned so far is the small part your light reaches.

19
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:05,424
Just past the light, you can make out a few things you have not learned yet.

20
00:01:05,464 --> 00:01:09,162
And beyond those, as far as you can tell, there is nothing.

21
00:01:09,202 --> 00:01:11,139
Call that line your fence.

22
00:01:11,576 --> 00:01:13,741
So when you ask yourself how much you know,

23
00:01:13,781 --> 00:01:16,793
you can only compare the light to what you can see.

24
00:01:16,833 --> 00:01:19,246
22 things out of 29.

25
00:01:19,368 --> 00:01:20,581
Most of it!

26
00:01:21,004 --> 00:01:22,879
Now let us lift the fog.

27
00:01:22,929 --> 00:01:25,339
The field was this big all along.

28
00:01:25,379 --> 00:01:28,104
You know 22 things out of 400.

29
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:30,948
And you cannot count what you cannot see.

30
00:01:30,988 --> 00:01:35,925
To notice how much is missing, you would need the very knowledge that is missing.

31
00:01:37,566 --> 00:01:39,341
Now, the whole journey.

32
00:01:39,495 --> 00:01:42,195
Gold is how much of the field you feel you know.

33
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:44,580
Blue is how much you really know.

34
00:01:44,736 --> 00:01:48,948
With the first lessons, the feeling races ahead: a small light,

35
00:01:48,988 --> 00:01:50,948
in a field that looks small.

36
00:01:50,988 --> 00:01:54,963
This is the first peak of confidence, and you have barely started.

37
00:01:55,367 --> 00:01:58,564
Then every answer shows you three new questions.

38
00:01:58,604 --> 00:02:03,992
The fence runs away faster than your light can follow, and your confidence falls.

39
00:02:05,121 --> 00:02:08,966
But look at the field: the fence has reached the true edge.

40
00:02:09,006 --> 00:02:11,681
For the first time, you can see all of it.

41
00:02:12,101 --> 00:02:14,593
So the bottom is not where you know least.

42
00:02:14,633 --> 00:02:17,533
It is where you start being right about yourself.

43
00:02:17,959 --> 00:02:21,337
And from here on, the feeling and the facts climb together.

44
00:02:21,377 --> 00:02:23,239
Confidence comes back.

45
00:02:23,292 --> 00:02:25,229
This time, it is earned.

46
00:02:26,833 --> 00:02:31,393
At the other end, there is a smaller mistake, and it points the other way.

47
00:02:31,433 --> 00:02:34,658
Here is someone who has lit almost the whole field.

48
00:02:35,368 --> 00:02:40,228
When something has become easy for you, you assume it is easy for everyone.

49
00:02:40,268 --> 00:02:44,478
So the expert pictures everybody else with a light as big as their own,

50
00:02:44,518 --> 00:02:49,293
and places themselves above the middle of the pack, but well short of the top.

51
00:02:49,704 --> 00:02:52,177
In truth, most lights are small.

52
00:02:52,217 --> 00:02:56,805
Line everyone up by what they really know, and the expert stands at the very top,

53
00:02:56,845 --> 00:02:58,345
without knowing it.

54
00:02:58,893 --> 00:03:01,168
So beginners misjudge themselves.

55
00:03:01,216 --> 00:03:03,478
Experts misjudge everyone else.

56
00:03:04,950 --> 00:03:07,698
And none of this is about foolish people.

57
00:03:07,738 --> 00:03:12,085
Each of us carries many lights: a big one for what we do every day,

58
00:03:12,125 --> 00:03:14,975
and small ones for almost everything else.

59
00:03:15,383 --> 00:03:20,508
And from the inside, a small light can feel almost as complete as a big one.

60
00:03:20,903 --> 00:03:23,988
A small light cannot show you where the field ends.

61
00:03:24,028 --> 00:03:25,593
Learning more does.

62
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:30,108
So keep learning, and ask people who know more to teach you what you are missing.

63
00:03:30,768 --> 00:03:33,577
Because feeling sure tells you about your light.

64
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:37,129
On its own, it cannot tell you the size of the dark.
